-- GAME ----------------------------------------------------------------------
  Title       Match Day
  Publisher   Ocean, 1984
  Version     2.1

-- YOU NEED ------------------------------------------------------------------
  Your own copy of the original game, saved beside this patch:

  File        Match Day (1984)(Ocean).z80
  MD5         d5ea19e487141ce922d0e96c02fd9054

  The patch carries none of the game's code. It only works with that exact
  dump, under that name or any other: anything else is refused, with a
  message naming the file it wants.

-- WHAT IT DOES --------------------------------------------------------------
  Moving figures become Next hardware sprites, so they keep their own colours
  and no longer take on the scenery's (colour clash). Game logic is unchanged.

  Pace        original (-0.61% measured in JNext 1.1.104 headless, the plain
              .nex (02fd1835) as control, input keyed to the match loop
              0x817F, lockstep held (one busy trial measured to update 236,
              where the games part); the hooks restore the incoming speed and
              a limiter holds the game's own code run at 28 MHz (image builds,
              XOR draw, the figures' calls, crowd copier, ball) to the
              original's T on the video line, interrupt time left out, paid
              once per match-loop pass; fastest third of updates +2.0 to
              +5.7%, slowest -0.2 to -4.2%)

  Changes     1.0: Dean Woodyatt's build f0aecd13
              2.0: whole alt ROM image, start-up tail at 0xBE00 (the trophy
              screen clean)
              2.1: original pace; hooks restore the game's speed

  Note        Dean Woodyatt's Match Day engine with the whole alt ROM image
              and the start-up tail moved off the trophy screen's data: the
              trophy after the title is drawn clean, and the menus no longer
              depend on the Next's own ROM. Players, ball and keepers are
              hardware sprites in the game's colours. Plays at the original's
              pace, and the hooks give the CPU back at the speed they found
              it. Known issues: none found in testing. Missing: no loading
              screen (the build already uses the last bank of a 1MB Next); no
              cheats (the archive's POKEs only ask for values); not yet
              checked: the second half, 2-player games and Match Day Special;
              hardware untested.

-- MENU ----------------------------------------------------------------------
